# Auto-resolve merge conflicts in generated arch artifacts.
#
# docs/arch/.meta.json and docs/arch/generated/changelog.md are regenerated by
# `arch.runner --emit` on essentially every commit — the changelog grows from
# `git log`, and .meta.json carries a fresh timestamp + HEAD sha. So any two
# branches that both ran arch-regen conflict on them, forcing endless manual
# re-resolution on every staging advance. Neither file is drift-checked
# (arch.runner exempts changelog.md; .meta.json is not an arch artifact), and
# both are re-derived deterministically on the mainline by DiagramLoop /
# arch-regen.yml — so it is always safe to auto-resolve them at merge time.
#
# The OTHER generated artifacts under docs/arch/generated/ (loops.md, ports.md,
# …) are deliberately NOT listed here: they ARE drift-checked, so a conflict in
# them signals a real content change that must be resolved by hand.

# changelog.md is no longer tracked (see .gitignore): it is a view of `git log`,
# so its bytes are a function of the branch's commit graph and it can never be
# conflict-free while committed. `merge=union` papered over that by duplicating
# entries. It is now rendered at Pages-deploy time instead, which removes the
# conflict class rather than resolving it.

# .meta.json: cannot use `union` (it would corrupt the JSON) and its commit_sha
# is load-bearing for staleness detection, so it stays committed + current on
# the mainline. `merge=arch-meta` resolves a conflict to the incoming (staging)
# copy — the driver is registered by `make ensure-hooks`. A feature branch that
# carries a slightly-stale .meta.json is harmless (not drift-checked; the badge
# is cosmetic off-mainline) and DiagramLoop refreshes it after the squash-merge.
# Taking the incoming copy keeps the branch's .meta.json tracking staging, so
# `ours == merge-base` on the next advance and GitHub's server-side merge stays
# conflict-free too.
docs/arch/.meta.json merge=arch-meta
